    objectives: ['Create server actions with the use server directive', 'Handle form mutations with server actions', 'Implement optimistic updates with server actions'],
    topics: [
      { id: 'server-actions-basics', title: 'Server Actions Basics', content: 'Server Actions are async functions that run on the server and can be called from client components. Mark a function with the use server directive at the top of the file or inline. Server actions can be called from form submissions using the action prop or from event handlers using startTransition. The server executes the function and returns the result to the client. Server actions handle form data natively — they accept FormData objects and can read form fields with get() or entries(). The redirect function navigates to a different page after the action completes. Server actions simplify mutation logic by eliminating the need for API endpoints. They integrate with Next.js caching to automatically revalidate relevant pages after mutations.' },
      { id: 'form-mutations', title: 'Form Mutations', content: 'Server actions integrate seamlessly with HTML forms. Pass the server action to the form action prop and React handles the submission lifecycle. The form disables native submission and uses fetch under the hood. While the action is pending the form is in a loading state — you can check this with useFormStatus which returns pending boolean. After success the action can redirect to another page or return data for optimistic updates. Server actions receive the form data as a FormData argument, making form handling straightforward. Use useActionState (or useFormState in older versions) to access the action return value and error state in the component. The combination of server actions and useFormStatus provides a complete form handling solution without client-side state management.' },
      { id: 'optimistic-server-actions', title: 'Optimistic Server Actions', content: 'Optimistic updates with server actions use useOptimistic to immediately update the UI before the server confirms the change. The useOptimistic hook takes the current state and returns [optimisticState, addOptimistic]. Call addOptimistic inside startTransition wrapping the server action to apply the optimistic update. When the server action completes React replaces the optimistic state with the actual result. If the action fails React rolls back to the previous state. This creates instant UI feedback while maintaining data consistency. Combine with useTransition to show loading states during the action. The optimistic state is transient and does not affect the underlying data source until the server confirms. This pattern works well for likes, comments, cart operations, and any action where instant feedback improves the user experience.' }
    ],
